Back to Articles Welcome Muse Spark Open: open-weight frontier multimodal mixture-of-experts model from Meta Published October 15, 2026 Update on GitHub Upvote 19 +13 Pedro Cuenca pcuenq Follow merve merve Follow ben burtenshaw burtenshaw Follow Guarin Flück guarin Follow Quentin Gallouédec qgallouedec Follow Alvaro Bartolome alvarobartt Follow Serveurperso Serveurperso Follow Jun Kim Jundot Follow Muse Spark Open is an open-weight model from Meta. The mixture of experts model brings text, images, audio, and video into a model with approximately 816 billion parameters, around 42 billion active per token, and a context window of up to one million tokens. It generates text, giving developers a common interface for asking questions about different kinds of input. Following Muse Glimmer, Muse Spark Open introduces a mixture-of-experts architecture at a much larger scale, and the most capable US open-weights model to date. Meta trained Muse Spark Open for agentic coding: reading a codebase, writing and debugging code, and calling tools. It also takes images, audio, and video as input, so the same model can read a screenshot, listen to a voice note, or watch a screen recording while it works on your code. This post shows you how to run it, how to fine-tune it, and what we built with it. Find all the models here . Overall Capabilities and Architecture Meta describes Muse Spark Open as a coding-optimized model trained on real developer workflows such as multi-file refactors and long debugging sessions. Muse Spark Open is an agentic focused model that plans, acts, observes, and self-corrects over long tasks, including browsing the web with images. Feature Muse Spark Open Model size ~816B total parameters; ~42B active per token Expert routing 256 experts per MoE layer; 8 selected per token Inputs Text, images, audio, and video Output Text Context window Up to 1M tokens Checkpoint formats BF16 and NVFP4 The checkpoint repositories are Muse-Spark-Open-816B-A42B and Muse-Spark-Open-816B-A42B-NVFP4. Muse Spark Open is the full-size model of the Muse series of models. Muse Glimmer, which Meta released in August, is a small dense model from the same series, ideal for running locally. Both models use the same tokenizer and vocabulary. The chat template follows the Muse Glimmer template, with additions for the new modalities. In Muse Spark Open the input embedding and the output layer are separate matrices of about 1.66B parameters each. Mixture of Experts The language model has 62 layers and a hidden dimension of 8192. Each layer has 128 query heads and 16 key-value heads (grouped-query attention). Each head has 64 dimensions. The attention block has an output gate and an output normalization layer. The model also applies normalization to the queries and the keys (QK-norm). Each block combines its input and its output through a learned residual gate, not through a plain sum. Muse Glimmer uses one dense feed-forward network in each layer. Muse Spark Open replaces this dense network with a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) block. Each MoE block has 256 experts, and a router selects 8 experts for each token. Each expert is a feed-forward network that projects from 8192 to 4096 dimensions and back to 8192, then applies its own normalization to its output. The experts hold ~97% of the total model parameters. Only 8 experts run per token, so the model computes each token with about 42B parameters. All 256 experts stay in memory, so deployment needs the memory of an 816B-parameter model, but runs at the much faster speed of a 42B model. The BF16 checkpoint is about 1.5 TB in safetensors format. The NVFP4 checkpoint stores the parameters in 4-bit precision with negligible loss in accuracy, specifically for NVIDIA Blackwell GPUs. In transformers, the BF16 checkpoint runs across 4 nodes of 8 H200 GPUs with tensor parallelism (TP=32) and the NVFP4 runs on a single B200 node. If you want to deploy Muse Spark Open, check out the llama.cpp , vLLM, and SGLang recipes below. Perception Encoder A perception encoder converts images and video frames into tokens for the language model, just like Muse Glimmer. The encoder has 1.8B parameters, 50 layers, a hidden dimension of 1536, and 16 attention heads. It splits each image into 14×14 patches and produces features with 4096 dimensions. Position information comes from learned absolute embeddings and 2D RoPE. The feed-forward network in each layer uses LayerNorm and GELU. Every fourth layer and the last layer use full attention, and the other layers use sparse attention. Muse Glimmer 30B uses an encoder with the same architecture. The design follows the Perception Encoder work from Meta. Video input goes through the same encoder as images. Audio Encoder Muse Spark Open accepts audio as input through its audio encoder. An audio tokenizer converts the audio into tokens, and the chat template marks the audio position with <|audio|> tokens. The encoder emits one audio token every 80 ms, which yields 12.5 tokens per second. So let’s say, you have an audio of 10 seconds, the number of input audio tokens would be 125.
The encoder itself is a convolutional pyramid where each stage consists of a patch embedder of different patch sizes and 2 convolutional blocks of 1d kernels of size 7 with stride of 1. These stages are repeated for patch sizes of [16, 6, 5, 4]. At the end of the final stage, the temporal frames are fed to a final block of RMSNorm → linear projection → RMSNorm. Long Context The context window is 1M tokens at inference. The configuration sets max_position_embeddings to 1,048,576. This includes image, video, and audio tokens in the same window as text. Grouped-query attention keeps the KV cache small for a model of this size. Each token adds 2 × 16 heads × 64 dimensions × 62 layers = 126,976 values to the cache. In BF16, this is about 250 KB per token and about 266 GB for a full 1M-token context. Inference Support Transformers The Muse Spark Open implementation provides MuseSparkForConditionalGeneration for text generation from multimodal inputs and MuseSparkProcessor for preparing conversations. The processor combines the tokenizer, image and video processors, audio feature extractor, and chat template. Use a DistributedConfig to configure the parallelism settings. tp_size is the number of devices across which the dense layers are sharded, and ep_size is the number of devices across which the experts are distributed. You can initialize the model and processor as follows. import os from transformers import AutoModelForMultimodalLM, AutoTokenizer, DistributedConfig distributed_config = DistributedConfig( tp_size= int (os.environ[ "WORLD_SIZE" ]), ep_size= int (os.environ[ "WORLD_SIZE" ]),
) tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained( "meta-models/Muse-Spark-Open-816B-A42B " )
model = AutoModelForMultimodalLM.from_pretrained( "meta-models/Muse-Spark-Open-816B-A42B" , distributed_config=distributed_config,
) messages = [ { "role" : "user" , "content" : "Explain in one sentence why the sky is usually blue." },
]
inputs = tokenizer.apply_chat_template( messages, reasoning_effort= "medium" , # default: xhigh, options: low/medium/high/xhigh add_generation_prompt= True , tokenize= True , return_dict= True , return_tensors= "pt" ,
).to(model.device).to(model.dtype) input_len = inputs[ "input_ids" ].shape[- 1 ] outputs = model.generate(**inputs, max_new_tokens= 2000 ) Text-only Inference Prepare the conversation, then pass the messages to processor.apply_chat_template , enabling add_generation_prompt=True , tokenize=True , return_dict=True , and return_tensors="pt" . Pass the processor output to model.generate and decode the newly generated tokens, excluding the input prefix. The processor has a lever for the reasoning effort, mapping symbolic levels (low/medium/high/xhigh) to the integers the model itself accepts (16, 64, 256, 512).

Meta's Muse Spark Open is available on Together AI today. We're a Day 0 launch partner for the most capable US open-weight model to date, built for long-running agentic coding workflows that keep entire codebases in context. Muse Spark Open is a step forward for agentic software engineering, computer use and web automation, and understanding documents, images, audio, and video together. Through our early access program, we offered it to select customers before launch. They evaluated it, prepared to offer it to their own customers on day 0, and shared real-world feedback with Meta. Read Meta's announcement . About Muse Spark Open Muse Spark Open is trained on real developer work: multi-file refactors, debugging sessions, and long-running tasks. The result is higher first-attempt accuracy, reliable tool calling, and deep codebase understanding across the repositories developers actually work in. What stands out: 1M-token context window. It holds entire projects, including complex dependencies, legacy code, and thousands of files, without splitting work into pieces. Long-horizon autonomy. It plans, acts, observes, and self-corrects over long horizons, and coordinates subagents for work that can be split up. Computer use and browsing. It uses computer-use capabilities across desktop, mobile, and browser, and browses the web with multimodal browsing. Multimodal input. It accepts text, image, audio, and audio-video input. Intent commentary. It states what it is doing and why before acting, which makes long runs easier to audit. What early access customers said Factory and Ollama were two customers with early access. Both used Muse Spark Open before launch, and both offer it to their users from day 0. "Enterprises choose Factory because they want their Droids running on the best model for the task. In our agentic engineering workflows, Muse Spark Open consistently selected the right tool and kept its responses compact, exactly the decisiveness production agents need. Early access through Together AI let us stress-test it ahead of launch, so our customers get access on day 0." Zimu Li, Member of technical staff, Factory "Millions of developers across the globe rely on Ollama as the destination to get up and running with open source models. We are delighted to offer Muse Spark Open in partnership with Together AI. This model performs particularly well on tool calling and long context, including multi-step and parallel calls with clean structured outputs. Partnering with Together AI for early access allowed us to sufficiently evaluate and optimize the model so that developers experienced a fully supported, production-ready model from the moment it launched." Dong Chen, Senior AI Engineer, Ollama Run it on Together AI Serverless. Call Muse Spark Open with the Together API: from together import Together client = Together() response = client.chat.completions.create( model="meta-models/Muse-Spark-Open-816B-A42B", messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Refactor this module to remove the circular import."}],
)
print(response.choices[0].message.content) Pricing is $1.25 per 1M input tokens, $4.25 per 1M output tokens, and $0.15 per 1M cached input tokens. You can also try it in the playground. Get started or ‍ Talk to an engineer ‍

MCP for Blender Connect Blender to any LLM formerly blender-mcp — the PyPI package is now mcp-for-blender . Existing setups keep working; no config change is required. Read more Disclaimer: This is a third-party integration and not made by Blender Prompt-assisted 3D modeling, scene creation, and manipulation — driven by AI. Supporters CodeRabbit Guillermo Rauch All supporters: Support this project Quickstart Note: the PyPI package blender-mcp is now mcp-for-blender . Existing setups
keep working — uvx blender-mcp still runs the server and no config change is
required . New installs should use mcp-for-blender . What changed and why One command sets up your AI apps and the Blender addon: macOS / Linux curl -LsSf https://www.mcp-for-blender.com/install.sh | sh Windows (PowerShell) powershell - ExecutionPolicy ByPass - c " irm https://www.mcp-for-blender.com/install.ps1 | iex " Already have uv? Run uvx mcp-for-blender setup instead. It installs uv if needed, finds the MCP clients on your machine (Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, VS Code, Devin Desktop/Windsurf, OpenCode, Antigravity), and lets you pick which to set up. Then it installs the Blender addon and enables it. Your existing config is kept: it only adds a blender entry and saves a .bak of each file it changes. Then fully quit and reopen your AI app (on Windows, quit it from the system tray), open Blender, and ask it to build something. The addon starts its server when Blender opens. Manual setup, in case the automatic setup doesn't work Three steps: install uv , point your MCP client at the server, install the Blender addon. 1. Install uv # macOS brew install uv # Linux curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh # Windows powershell -c " irm https://astral.sh/uv/install.ps1 | iex " Warning: Do not proceed before installing uv. Use the official installer — not pip install uv . 2. Add the MCP server to your client Claude Desktop — Settings → Developer → Edit Config { "mcpServers" : { "blender" : { "command" : " uvx " , "args" : [ " mcp-for-blender " ]
        }
    }
} Claude Code claude mcp add blender uvx mcp-for-blender Codex codex mcp add blender -- uvx mcp-for-blender Cursor / VS Code / OpenCode / Antigravity See MCP Client Setup below for per-client instructions and one-click install buttons. 3. Install the Blender addon uvx mcp-for-blender install-addon Then in Blender: Edit → Preferences → Add-ons → enable Interface: MCP for Blender . 4. Connect The addon starts its server when Blender opens. To check, press N in the 3D viewport → open the MCP for Blender tab (click Start MCP Server if it isn't running). That's it — ask Claude to build something. Note: Only run one instance of the MCP server (either Cursor or Claude Desktop), not both. Table of Contents Quickstart Features Premium Components Installation Automatic setup Prerequisites Make your client find uvx Pin the Python version Install without uv Run with Docker Environment Variables MCP Client Setup Claude for Desktop Codex Cursor Visual Studio Code OpenCode Antigravity Installing the Blender Addon Upgrading (existing users) Usage Starting the Connection Using with Claude Capabilities Example Commands Persistent API Credentials Troubleshooting Technical Details Limitations & Security Considerations Telemetry Control Feedback Contributing Disclaimer Star History Features Two-way communication Connect Claude AI to Blender through a socket-based server Object manipulation Create, modify, and delete 3D objects in Blender Material control Apply and modify materials and colors Visual verification Multi-angle, camera, wireframe, X-ray and animation-strip views, plus renders, so the AI checks its own work Code execution The AI writes Blender Python directly Asset & model generation Poly Haven assets, Sketchfab models, Poly Pizza low-poly models, and AI-generated 3D models via Tripo, Hyper3D Rodin and Hunyuan3D Premium Generate AI 3D models (Hunyuan3D, Tripo, Hyper3D Rodin) straight into Blender without bringing your own API keys. More details Components The system consists of two main components: Blender Addon ( addon.py ) — a Blender addon that creates a socket server within Blender to receive and execute commands MCP Server ( src/blender_mcp/server.py ) — a Python server that implements the Model Context Protocol and connects to the Blender addon Installation Automatic setup The quickest way is the one-line installer. It installs uv, configures your MCP clients, and installs and enables the Blender addon: # macOS / Linux curl -LsSf https://www.mcp-for-blender.com/install.sh | sh # Windows (PowerShell) powershell - ExecutionPolicy ByPass - c " irm https://www.mcp-for-blender.com/install.ps1 | iex " Already have uv? Run uvx mcp-for-blender setup instead. See Quickstart for what it changes. The rest of this section covers manual setup. Prerequisites Blender 3.0 or newer Python 3.10 or newer uv package manager Installing uv, per platform macOS brew install uv Windows powershell - c " irm https://astral.sh/uv/install.ps1 | iex " Then add uv to the user path in Windows (you may need to restart Claude Desktop after): $localBin = " $ env: USERPROFILE \.local\bin " $userPath = [ Environment ]::GetEnvironmentVariable( " Path " , " User " )
[ Environment ]::SetEnvironmentVariable( " Path " , " $userPath ; $localBin " , " User " ) Linux curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh It lands in ~/.local/bin — open a new shell so it's on your PATH. Otherwise, installation instructions are on their website: Install uv On every OS, use uv's official installer above — not pip install uv , which may not create the uvx command and can hide uv inside an environment your client can't see. Warning: Do not proceed before installing uv. Make your client find uvx MCP clients started from a GUI (Claude Desktop, Cursor, VS Code from the Dock/Start menu) do not inherit your terminal's PATH, so a bare "command": "uvx" can fail with spawn uvx ENOENT even though uvx works in your terminal. If that happens: Find uvx's full path — which uvx (macOS/Linux) or where uvx (Windows) — and use it as "command" , e.g. /opt/homebrew/bin/uvx or C:\Users\<you>\.local\bin\uvx.exe . On Windows you can instead wrap it: "command": "cmd", "args": ["/c", "uvx", "mcp-for-blender"] . After any PATH or config change, fully quit and relaunch the client (Windows: quit from the system tray, not just the window; macOS: Cmd + Q ). Pin the Python version Avoid conda / pyenv / version conflicts. uv chooses which Python runs the server. On machines with conda (auto-activated base), pyenv, or asdf — or with a newer CPython release that some dependencies do not have wheels for yet — uv can grab an interpreter that makes installation fail. Pin Python 3.11 and prefer uv-managed interpreters to avoid using whatever is on your PATH: { "mcpServers" : { "blender" : { "command" : " uvx " , "args" : [ " --python " , " 3.11 " , " mcp-for-blender " ], "env" : { "UV_PYTHON_PREFERENCE" : " only-managed " }
        }
    }
} --python 3.11 still satisfies this package's requires-python >=3.10 , and UV_PYTHON_PREFERENCE=only-managed keeps uv from selecting conda, pyenv, asdf, or system Python first. (The repo's .python-version is only a hint for contributors and does not affect uvx .) If a previous failed attempt keeps replaying after a fix, clear the cache: uv cache clean mcp-for-blender blender-mcp && uvx --refresh mcp-for-blender Install without uv On locked-down machines you can skip uvx entirely with pipx , then point your client at the installed command: pipx install mcp-for-blender
pipx ensurepath # then restart your shell / client Use the resulting absolute path as "command" (find it with which mcp-for-blender / where mcp-for-blender ) and omit args . Run with Docker You can run the MCP server in a container instead of installing it. Blender itself still runs on your machine — the container only hosts the MCP server, which connects out to the Blender addon.
